  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Evolving as a Digital Scholar is already showing practical reach: it has been downloaded 5,052 times through OAPEN, suggesting sustained use by readers exploring digital scholarship. The book has also begun to register in scholarly literature, with 2 citations, and it aligns with the SDG theme of industry, innovation and infrastructure. Funding support from KU Leuven is recorded in the dataset.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2021, Evolving as a Digital Scholar presents a framework for becoming a digitally agile academic across research, teaching, and service. Its impact record points first to readership and uptake: the book has accumulated 5,052 OAPEN downloads, indicating that it has found a substantial audience beyond its immediate publication context.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is still early, but it shows initial scholarly engagement. Two citing works have been identified, appearing in 2021 and 2025 and spanning both an article and a book chapter. The citing literature touches areas such as social work education, digital humanities, reflective practice, and educational innovation, which suggests that the book’s ideas are being taken up across adjacent fields rather than remaining confined to a single disciplinary niche.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The thematic profile of the book also supports that pattern of cross-cutting relevance. Its concepts include digital media, multimedia, social media, and narrative, alongside educational and scholarly practice themes. The record further indicates alignment with industry, innovation and infrastructure, reinforcing the book’s policy- and practice-oriented relevance around digital capability and scholarly transformation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals are more limited and should be read cautiously. The available record shows one attention mention in Crossref-linked sources, and there is no event data in the dataset. Taken together, the evidence points to a work that is already being read and is beginning to circulate in scholarship, with its longer-term influence still likely to develop as more citing literature appears.</p>",
